<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>Not a PNG</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>This is HTML, not a PNG.</h1>
    <p>
      The extension says .png and the browser types the file from its extension,
      so `file.type` is "image/png" and the client-side allow-list waves it
      through. The first eight bytes are "&lt;!doctyp", not the PNG signature,
      which is what the server's magic-byte check actually decides on.
    </p>
    <script>
      document.title = "if this ever executes, something served it as HTML"
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
